1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,774
A lightning bolt heats the air to
around thirty thousand degrees Celsius

2
00:00:04,814 --> 00:00:08,397
— about five times hotter
than the surface of the Sun.

3
00:00:08,437 --> 00:00:11,862
Now zoom out: an entire
city, glowing at night.

4
00:00:11,902 --> 00:00:15,967
Zoom in: the phone on your
bedside table, quietly charging.

5
00:00:16,007 --> 00:00:18,547
Wildly different scales… same thing.

6
00:00:18,587 --> 00:00:19,927
Electricity.

7
00:00:19,967 --> 00:00:21,717
We use it every day.

8
00:00:21,757 --> 00:00:24,197
But what actually makes it move?

9
00:00:29,183 --> 00:00:35,083
It starts with electrons: tiny, negatively
charged particles inside every atom.

10
00:00:35,123 --> 00:00:38,352
In metals like copper,
some electrons roam freely

11
00:00:38,392 --> 00:00:41,705
between atoms, making
metals good conductors.

12
00:00:41,745 --> 00:00:47,431
In rubber, glass or plastic, electrons
are held tightly — those are insulators.

13
00:00:47,471 --> 00:00:51,248
That's why wires are copper
inside and plastic outside.

14
00:00:51,288 --> 00:00:55,983
But for current to flow, charge
needs a complete path: a closed loop.

15
00:00:56,023 --> 00:00:59,093
Open a switch, and the
flow stops everywhere.

16
00:00:59,983 --> 00:01:03,280
So what pushes charge
around the loop? Voltage.

17
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:05,655
Think of a battery as a charge pump:

18
00:01:05,695 --> 00:01:10,030
chemical reactions give each
bit of charge a boost of energy.

19
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:13,367
Voltage is that energy per unit of charge.

20
00:01:13,407 --> 00:01:19,080
One volt is one joule per coulomb — the
charge of about six quintillion electrons.

21
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:22,084
Voltage is always a
difference between two points

22
00:01:22,124 --> 00:01:25,151
— that's why it's called
potential difference.

23
00:01:25,191 --> 00:01:27,955
A AA battery: one and a half volts.

24
00:01:27,995 --> 00:01:33,875
A wall socket: 120 or 230,
depending on where you live.

25
00:01:33,915 --> 00:01:37,102
Transmission lines: hundreds of thousands.

26
00:01:37,817 --> 00:01:41,517
Current is how much charge
flows past a point each second.

27
00:01:41,557 --> 00:01:45,064
One ampere, or amp,
is one coulomb per second.

28
00:01:45,214 --> 00:01:49,187
Early scientists guessed current
flows from positive to negative,

29
00:01:49,227 --> 00:01:52,747
and we still draw it that way:
conventional current.

30
00:01:52,787 --> 00:01:58,389
But in metal wires, the moving charges
are electrons, drifting the opposite way.

31
00:01:58,429 --> 00:02:00,851
Both pictures give the same answers.

32
00:02:00,891 --> 00:02:03,437
Surprisingly, those electrons are slow —

33
00:02:03,477 --> 00:02:07,601
a fraction of a millimetre per
second, slower than a snail.

34
00:02:07,641 --> 00:02:12,435
Yet the light comes on almost instantly:
the wire is already full of electrons,

35
00:02:12,475 --> 00:02:16,802
and the push races along it at
more than half the speed of light.

36
00:02:17,617 --> 00:02:21,326
Resistance is opposition to
current, measured in ohms

37
00:02:21,366 --> 00:02:24,647
— after Georg Ohm — with the symbol omega.

38
00:02:24,687 --> 00:02:26,237
Four things set it.

39
00:02:26,277 --> 00:02:31,877
Material: copper barely resists;
nichrome, used in toasters, resists a lot.

40
00:02:31,917 --> 00:02:34,595
Length: longer wire, more resistance.

41
00:02:34,635 --> 00:02:38,371
Thickness: thicker wire, less
— more lanes for traffic.

42
00:02:38,411 --> 00:02:41,876
Temperature: most metals
resist more as they heat up.

43
00:02:42,126 --> 00:02:47,261
Resistance is also useful: push current
through a high-resistance wire and it

44
00:02:47,301 --> 00:02:52,759
gets hot enough to glow — like a toaster
element or an old-style light bulb.

45
00:02:53,550 --> 00:02:57,540
Picture water in a closed loop
of pipe, driven by a pump.

46
00:02:57,580 --> 00:03:00,010
The pump's pressure is like voltage.

47
00:03:00,050 --> 00:03:02,644
The water's flow rate is like current.

48
00:03:02,684 --> 00:03:05,823
And a narrow section of
pipe is like resistance.

49
00:03:05,863 --> 00:03:09,221
More pressure, more flow;
narrower pipe, less flow.

50
00:03:09,471 --> 00:03:11,771
But here's where the analogy breaks.

51
00:03:11,811 --> 00:03:16,342
Cut a water pipe and water spills out;
cut a wire and current stops.

52
00:03:16,492 --> 00:03:20,224
And in a pipe, the water carries
the energy; in a circuit,

53
00:03:20,264 --> 00:03:25,037
energy travels through electric and
magnetic fields around the wire.

54
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:27,960
Ohm's law connects all three.

55
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,134
For many materials — like a
metal wire at steady temperature

56
00:03:32,174 --> 00:03:36,449
— current is proportional to
voltage: V equals I times R.

57
00:03:36,489 --> 00:03:40,682
Memory trick: draw a triangle
— V on top, I and R below.

58
00:03:40,722 --> 00:03:42,222
Cover what you want.

59
00:03:42,262 --> 00:03:44,332
Cover V: I times R.

60
00:03:44,372 --> 00:03:46,462
Cover I: V over R.

61
00:03:46,502 --> 00:03:48,702
Cover R: V over I.

62
00:03:48,952 --> 00:03:50,352
Example one.

63
00:03:50,392 --> 00:03:55,097
An ideal nine-volt battery is
connected to a three-ohm resistor.

64
00:03:55,137 --> 00:03:56,587
Find the current.

65
00:03:56,737 --> 00:04:01,101
I equals V over R:
nine divided by three… three amps.

66
00:04:01,551 --> 00:04:02,961
Example two.

67
00:04:03,001 --> 00:04:06,096
A small twelve-volt car
bulb draws half an amp.

68
00:04:06,136 --> 00:04:07,906
Find its resistance.

69
00:04:08,056 --> 00:04:14,170
R equals V over I: twelve divided by
zero point five… twenty-four ohms.

70
00:04:15,183 --> 00:04:18,916
Bonus formula: power —
how fast energy is delivered

71
00:04:18,956 --> 00:04:22,299
— equals voltage times current, in watts.

72
00:04:22,339 --> 00:04:26,096
Our nine-volt, three-amp
circuit? Twenty-seven watts.

73
00:04:26,136 --> 00:04:32,761
A kettle on a 230-volt socket draws
about ten amps — over two thousand watts.

74
00:04:32,801 --> 00:04:35,540
Big currents mean big
heating, so fuses and

75
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:38,798
breakers cut the power
before wires overheat.

76
00:04:38,838 --> 00:04:42,945
It's current through your body that
harms you — and voltage drives it.

77
00:04:42,985 --> 00:04:47,985
As little as a tenth of an amp
across the chest can be fatal.

78
00:04:48,025 --> 00:04:51,519
Never poke anything into sockets,
keep water away from

79
00:04:51,559 --> 00:04:55,181
electricity, and stay clear
of fallen power lines.

80
00:04:56,483 --> 00:04:58,223
Let's bust three myths.

81
00:04:58,263 --> 00:05:00,941
Myth one: batteries store electrons.

82
00:05:00,981 --> 00:05:06,027
They don't — they store chemical energy;
the electrons are already in the circuit.

83
00:05:06,067 --> 00:05:08,671
Myth two: a bulb uses up current.

84
00:05:08,711 --> 00:05:14,248
In a simple loop, the same current flows
in and out. What the bulb uses is energy.

85
00:05:14,288 --> 00:05:17,168
Myth three: voltage flows. It doesn't.

86
00:05:17,208 --> 00:05:20,971
Current flows through things;
voltage is across them.

87
00:05:21,883 --> 00:05:23,163
Quick recap.

88
00:05:23,203 --> 00:05:26,991
Voltage is the push:
energy per charge, in volts.

89
00:05:27,031 --> 00:05:30,391
Current is the flow:
charge per second, in amps.

90
00:05:30,431 --> 00:05:33,451
Resistance is the opposition, in ohms.

91
00:05:33,491 --> 00:05:37,203
And Ohm's law ties them
together: V equals I R.

92
00:05:37,553 --> 00:05:40,043
Your turn — pause if you need to.

93
00:05:40,083 --> 00:05:45,619
One: a twelve-volt battery is connected
to a six-ohm resistor. What's the current?

94
00:05:45,659 --> 00:05:49,681
Two: in the water analogy,
what does a narrow pipe represent?

95
00:05:49,721 --> 00:05:53,281
Three: true or false —
a bulb uses up current.

96
00:05:58,331 --> 00:05:59,471
Answers!

97
00:05:59,511 --> 00:06:02,511
One: twelve divided by six — two amps.

98
00:06:02,551 --> 00:06:04,131
Two: resistance.

99
00:06:04,171 --> 00:06:08,663
Three: false — the current is the same;
it's energy that's transferred.

100
00:06:08,703 --> 00:06:12,703
Nail all three, and you've got
the foundation of every circuit.
